    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Louis became king at four, lived through the Fronde as a child, and from 1661 ruled without a chief minister, drawing the nobility to his court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> and weakening their independent power.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was the leading European power under him, but his wars against shifting alliances strained its finances, and the long war over the Spanish succession ended in 1713 and 1714 with France in major debt.</li><li>He banned Protestant worship by the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> in 1685, after years of persecution and the quartering of dragoons in Protestant homes; historians still dispute how much the departure of some 200,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> harmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Louis was an eager dancer who performed 80 roles in 40 major ballets, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moliere">Molière</a>'s Les Amants magnifiques in 1670.</p></aside>
    </section>

