    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1638</span><span class="rf-text">Louis is born at Saint-Germain-en-Laye after 23 years of his parents' marriage and is named Louis Dieudonné</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1643</span><span class="rf-text">He becomes king at the age of four on the death of Louis XIII, with his mother Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> as sole regent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a> as chief minister</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1661</span><span class="rf-text">After Mazarin's death Louis declares that he will rule without a chief minister</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1672</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> invades the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a>, which ends with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1678</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1682</span><span class="rf-text">The royal court moves officially to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1685</span><span class="rf-text">The Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> revokes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-nantes">Edict of Nantes</a> and ends the toleration of Protestants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1713</span><span class="rf-text">The Peace of Utrecht ends the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, leaving Philip V on the Spanish throne</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1715</span><span class="rf-text">Louis dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> and is succeeded by his five-year-old great-grandson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin.jpg" alt="Jules Mazarin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Jules Mazarin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardinal_Mazarin_by_Pierre_Mignard_(Mus%C3%A9e_Cond%C3%A9)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pierre Mignard I, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Louis was born on 5 September 1638 at the Château de Saint-Germain-en-Laye, the son of Louis XIII and Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. His parents had been married for 23 years, and his mother had suffered four stillbirths between 1619 and 1631, so leading contemporaries regarded him as a divine gift and his birth as a miracle. He was named Louis Dieudonné, "Louis the God-given", and bore the title of the heir apparent, Dauphin. His relationship with his mother was uncommonly affectionate for the time, and it was she who gave him his belief in the absolute and divine power of his rule. In his childhood he was cared for by governesses, and in 1646 Nicolas V de Villeroy became his tutor.</p><p>Sensing his death near in the spring of 1643, Louis XIII decreed that a regency council would govern for his four-year-old son, with Anne at its head. He died on 14 May 1643. On 18 May Anne had the will annulled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a> and became sole regent. She appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a> chief minister and continued the policies of Louis XIII and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a>. In 1648 she and Mazarin negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a>, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands and claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-bishoprics">Three Bishoprics</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Louis XIV&#8221; cites where it covers Early years, Early acts, Coming of age and early reforms, Spain:</p><ul><li>John B. Wolf, Louis XIV (W.W. Norton &amp; Co., Inc., 1968)</li><li>John Lynn, The Wars of Louis XIV, 1667–1714 (Modern Wars in Perspective) (Longman, 1999)</li><li>Derek McKay, Small Power Diplomacy in the Age of Louis XIV. In Royal and Republican Sovereignty: Essays in Memory of Ragnhild Hatton (Cambridge University Press, 1997)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII of France<span>Father</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain.jpg" alt="Philip IV of Spain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV of Spain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_IV_of_Spain_-_Vel%C3%A1zquez_1644.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Velázquez, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The same year a civil war, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-fronde">Fronde</a>, broke out. When Anne and Mazarin tried in 1648 to tax the members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a>, the members refused and Mazarin had some of them arrested, among them Pierre Broussel; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> rioted and Anne had to free him. On the night of 9 to 10 February 1651, when Louis was twelve, a mob broke into the palace and was led into his bedchamber, where he feigned sleep. The royal family was driven out of Paris twice, and at one point Louis and Anne were held under virtual arrest in the palace. These years planted in Louis a hatred of Paris and a distrust of the higher aristocracy. The Fronde ended in 1653 when Mazarin returned from exile. Louis was declared of age on 7 September 1651. Anne and Mazarin sent away Mazarin's niece Marie Mancini, with whom Louis had fallen in love, and in 1660 he married Maria Theresa, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV of Spain</a>, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> of 1659.</p><p>When Mazarin died in March 1661, Louis astonished his court by declaring that he would govern himself: "It is now time that I govern them myself." He then moved against Nicolas Fouquet, the Superintendent of Finances, whose wealth and ambition worried him. Fouquet was charged with embezzlement and the Parlement sentenced him to exile; Louis altered the sentence to life imprisonment. In 1665 he named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a> Controller-General of Finances.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/canal-du-midi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canal-du-midi.jpg" alt="Canal du Midi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/canal-du-midi">Canal du Midi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pont-canal_de_l%27Orb004.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tournasol7, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-colbert">Colbert</a> reorganised taxation and put the accounts in order: the deficit of 1661 turned into a surplus by 1666, and interest on the debt fell from 52 million to 24 million livres. His mercantilist administration founded new industries, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> manufacturers and the Gobelins tapestry manufactory, and invited foreign craftsmen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Michel le Tellier and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-michel-le-tellier-marquis-de-louvois">Louvois</a> reorganised the army into a professional, disciplined force. The Great Ordinance of Civil Procedure of 1667, the Code Louis, imposed a uniform civil procedure across the kingdom and required baptismal, marriage and death records to be kept in state registers. The Great Ordinance on the Colonies of 1685, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-noir">Code Noir</a>, sanctioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> while prohibiting the separation of families, and allowed only Roman Catholics to own slaves.</p><p>Louis made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> the seat of government, and the court moved there officially on 6 May 1682. Nobles who wanted pensions and privileges had to wait constantly on the king, and he used court ritual and censorship to watch them, so that the old military aristocracy became ceremonial courtiers while commoners and the newer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobles-of-the-robe">nobility of the robe</a> filled executive posts. He patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moliere">Molière</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-racine">Racine</a>, La Fontaine, Lully, Le Brun and Le Nôtre, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canal-du-midi">Canal du Midi</a>, 240 km long, was built. His image as the Sun King was spread by portraits, medals, statues and tapestries, and Colbert set up a system to create it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Warfare defined Louis's foreign policy, impelled by his ambition for glory and power. He claimed Spanish territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-devolution">War of Devolution</a> of 1667 and invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> in May 1672; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> of 1678 left his two chief aims, destroying the Republic and conquering the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, unachieved. His annexations after 1679, including Strasbourg in 1681, alienated much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1684 he had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and Tripoli bombarded and sent a punitive mission against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, which began in 1688, French forces pursued a scorched earth policy in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the Devastation of the Palatinate, and in 1695 Louis ordered the bombardment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, which destroyed more than 4,000 buildings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> later called it "as barbarous as it was useless".</p><p>In 1681 Louis banned Protestant emigration and began quartering dragoons in Protestant homes, which subjected Protestants to ruin and atrocious abuse; between 300,000 and 400,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> converted. On 15 October 1685 the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a> revoked the Edict of Nantes and ordered Protestant churches demolished. Many historians condemn it as gravely harmful to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, citing the flight of about 200,000 Huguenots; others call this an exaggeration, arguing that most leading Protestant businessmen converted and stayed. Saint-Simon criticised Louis's love of flattery, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> held that he wanted glory more than conquests themselves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain.jpg" alt="Charles II of Spain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_de_Miranda_Carreno_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Juan Carreño de Miranda, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> died childless, leaving his empire to Louis's grandson Philip, Duke of Anjou. After weighing the partition treaties he had signed with William III, Louis accepted the will, and Anjou became Philip V. Louis then acknowledged James Stuart as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the death of James II, which enraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, and in 1702 they and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> declared war. The generals Marlborough and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Eugene of Savoy</a> checked French victories at Blenheim, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies">Ramillies</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-oudenarde">Oudenarde</a>. Between 1693 and 1710 over two million people died in two famines, made worse as armies foraged in the villages. By 1708 to 1709 Louis was willing to accept peace at nearly any cost, but refused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>' demand that he attack his own grandson. The Peace of Utrecht of 1713 and the treaties of 1714 left Philip V in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, gave Britain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a>, Rupert's Land and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in major debt.</p><p>The deaths of his son in 1711 and of two of his grandsons and a great-grandson within about a year left a five-year-old great-grandson as heir. Louis died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> of gangrene on 1 September 1715, reciting a psalm. After his death the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> had his will, which limited the regent's power, annulled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Louis XIV&#8221; cites where it covers Causes and build-up to the war, Acceptance of the will of Charles II and consequences, Commencement of fighting, Turning point:</p><ul><li>John Lynn, The Wars of Louis XIV, 1667–1714 (Modern Wars in Perspective) (Longman, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz.jpg" alt="Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christoph_Bernhard_Francke_-_Bildnis_des_Philosophen_Leibniz_(ca._1695).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Christoph Bernhard Francke, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Louis's tomb at Saint-Denis was opened by revolutionaries after about 80 years, and the remains destroyed. His admirers among historians point to the expansion and more defensible frontiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the reduction of aristocratic rebellion through his consolidation of royal authority, and the French culture that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> came to emulate; they hold that his early reforms marked the birth of the modern French state. His detractors argue that his expenditure impoverished and bankrupted France, and that his failure to reform institutions led to the Revolution; his defenders reply that the state, not France, was impoverished, and that the Revolution came almost 80 years after his death.</p><p>According to Philippe de Courcillon's Journal, he told his heir on his deathbed, "I have often undertaken war too lightly and have sustained it for vanity", though some historians point out that exaggerating one's sins was a customary show of piety. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> called him "the only King of France worthy of the name", <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a> commended him, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>'s The Age of Louis XIV named his reign one of the four great ages of reason and culture. The saying "I am the state" was denounced as apocryphal by the early 19th century. In 1693 he founded the Order of Saint Louis, the first decoration that could be granted to non-nobles.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/te-deum">Te Deum<span>c. 380</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Louis XIV (1638–1715) became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at four and ruled in person from 1661, building the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> and subduing the nobility. France led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> under him, but his wars, the persecution of Protestants and the costs of the Spanish succession war left deep debts and lasting dispute among historians.</em></p>

